The command that turns Cypress from a pure UI tool into something that can talk to your backend directly — and the foundation of the single highest-leverage habit in this entire manual: not testing through the UI what doesn't need to be tested through the UI.

## What cy.request() actually is

`cy.request()` makes a real HTTP request from outside the browser's page context — it runs in the Node-adjacent Cypress process, not inside the app iframe's JavaScript the way fetch() calls in your application code do. This is a genuinely different mechanism from `cy.intercept()`: `cy.intercept()` watches and controls requests your app makes; `cy.request()` is Cypress itself making a request, independent of whatever's rendered on screen.

```js
cy.request('GET', '/api/employees');
cy.request('/api/employees');                          // GET is the default method
cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', { type: 'sick', start: '2026-10-01' });

cy.request({
  method: 'POST',
  url: '/api/leave-requests',
  body: { type: 'sick', start: '2026-10-01', end: '2026-10-02' },
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
  qs: { source: 'test' },
  failOnStatusCode: true,
  timeout: 15000,
});
```

Because it resolves against `baseUrl` (Part 1.5) exactly like `cy.visit()` does, relative paths work identically across environments.

## The response object, in full

```js
cy.request('GET', '/api/employees').then((response) => {
  expect(response.status).to.eq(200);
  expect(response.statusText).to.eq('OK');
  expect(response.headers['content-type']).to.include('application/json');
  expect(response.body).to.have.length(10);
  expect(response.duration).to.be.lessThan(1000);
  expect(response.isOkStatusCode).to.be.true;
});
```

Cypress automatically parses JSON responses into response.body as a real object — no manual JSON.parse() needed, unlike raw fetch().

## Built-in retry-adjacent assertion: failing fast on bad status codes

By default, `cy.request()` fails the test immediately if the response status isn't 2xx or 3xx — this is the same philosophy as `cy.visit()` from Part 2.2, and for the same reason: a 500 from a setup call should produce an immediate, clear failure, not a confusing cascade of "element not found" errors five commands later when the UI never got the data it expected.

```js
cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', invalidPayload);
// If this returns 400, the test fails HERE, with the actual response body in the error —
// not twelve lines later when cy.visit() renders an empty page
```

To deliberately test an error response, opt out:

```js
cy.request({
  method: 'POST',
  url: '/api/leave-requests',
  body: { type: 'invalid-type' },
  failOnStatusCode: false,
}).then((response) => {
  expect(response.status).to.eq(400);
  expect(response.body.error).to.include('Invalid leave type');
});
```

## Setup — seeding data before a UI test, the core pattern

This is the pattern that appeared in preview form back in Part 3.2 as "the faster alternative you should know about," and it's the entire reason `cy.request()` deserves its own Part rather than being a footnote under Network commands.

```js
it('shows a newly created leave request in the approvals list', () => {
  cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', {
    employeeId: 123,
    type: 'sick',
    start: '2026-10-01',
    end: '2026-10-02',
    reason: 'Flu',
  }).then((response) => {
    const leaveId = response.body.id;

    cy.loginAs('manager');
    cy.visit('/leave/approvals');

    cy.contains('[data-cy=leave-row]', 'Flu')
      .should('have.attr', 'data-leave-id', String(leaveId));
  });
});
```

Compare this to the UI-driven alternative — navigate to the form, select a leave type, type two dates, type a reason, click submit, wait for the toast — none of which is the actual point of this test. The test is about the approvals list correctly displaying a pending request, not about the submission form. Every UI interaction not directly relevant to what's being verified is wasted time and an additional point of flakiness.

## Teardown — cleaning up what a test created

```js
describe('Leave request deletion', () => {
  let createdLeaveId;

  beforeEach(() => {
    cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', { type: 'sick', start: '2026-10-01' })
      .then((res) => { createdLeaveId = res.body.id; });
  });

  afterEach(() => {
    if (createdLeaveId) {
      cy.request({ method: 'DELETE', url: `/api/leave-requests/${createdLeaveId}`, failOnStatusCode: false });
    }
  });

  it('deletes the leave request from the UI', () => {
    cy.visit(`/leave/${createdLeaveId}`);
    cy.get('[data-cy=delete-btn]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=confirm]').click();
    cy.wait('@deleteLeave');
  });
});
```

Recall the warning from Part 2.1: after/afterEach cleanup is not guaranteed to run if the runner is stopped or a prior hook throws catastrophically. For data that genuinely must not leak between runs, the more robust pattern is "clean before, not clean after" — reset state in before/beforeEach rather than relying on afterEach alone:

```js
before(() => {
  cy.task('resetTestDatabase');    // guaranteed-clean starting point, regardless of prior run's fate
});
```

Using `cy.task()` for a full database reset (Part 1.9) is often more reliable than API-level teardown for anything beyond a single record, since it doesn't depend on remembering every ID created across a whole spec file.

## The single highest-leverage use: bypassing the login UI

This was previewed mechanically in Part 3.10; here it belongs properly, as an API-testing pattern in its own right:

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('loginAs', (role) => {
  cy.fixture('users').then((users) => {
    const { email, password } = users[role];

    cy.request('POST', '/api/auth/login', { email, password }).then((response) => {
      const { token, refreshToken, user } = response.body;

      window.localStorage.setItem('authToken', token);
      window.localStorage.setItem('refreshToken', refreshToken);
      cy.wrap(user).as('currentUser');
    });
  });
});
```

Wait — note the subtlety here worth flagging explicitly: setting localStorage directly via `window.localStorage` before any `cy.visit()` doesn't persist the way `onBeforeLoad` does, because Cypress's browser context resets storage between navigations under `testIsolation`. The reliable version threads it through `onBeforeLoad`:

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('loginAs', (role) => {
  cy.fixture('users').then((users) => {
    const { email, password } = users[role];
    cy.request('POST', '/api/auth/login', { email, password }).then((response) => {
      cy.wrap(response.body).as('authResponse');
    });
  });
});

Cypress.Commands.add('visitAuthenticated', (path) => {
  cy.get('@authResponse').then(({ token }) => {
    cy.visit(path, {
      onBeforeLoad(win) {
        win.localStorage.setItem('authToken', token);
      },
    });
  });
});

it('shows the dashboard for a logged-in employee', () => {
  cy.loginAs('employee');
  cy.visitAuthenticated('/dashboard');
  cy.get('[data-cy=welcome-banner]').should('be.visible');
});
```

The performance arithmetic here is worth stating concretely: a UI login typically costs 3–5 seconds (page load, type email, type password, click, wait for redirect). An API login costs 100–300ms. Across a suite of 150 tests that each need an authenticated session, that's the difference between roughly ten minutes and roughly half a minute of pure login overhead — and that gap only widens as the suite grows. This exact mechanism, with the caching layer that avoids even repeating the API call, is Part 7.2 (`cy.session()`) and Part 7.4 (Authentication) in full — what belongs here is the raw `cy.request()` mechanics that those chapters build on.
For cookie-based (rather than token-based) auth, it's even simpler — no `onBeforeLoad` juggling needed, because `cy.request()` running in the same browser context automatically applies any Set-Cookie response header:

```js
Cypress.Commands.add('loginAs', (role) => {
  cy.fixture('users').then((users) => {
    cy.request('POST', '/api/auth/login', users[role]);
    // the session cookie is now set in the browser automatically — no manual step needed
  });
});

cy.loginAs('manager');
cy.visit('/dashboard');   // already authenticated
```

## Combining cy.request() with cy.intercept() — a genuinely powerful pairing

Seed real data via the API, then also spy on the UI's subsequent fetch of that same data to assert the request shape is correct:

```js
it('requests the newly created leave with the right query params', () => {
  cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', { type: 'sick', start: '2026-10-01' })
    .then((res) => {
      cy.intercept('GET', `/api/leave-requests/${res.body.id}`).as('getLeave');
      cy.visit(`/leave/${res.body.id}`);
      cy.wait('@getLeave').its('response.statusCode').should('eq', 200);
    });
});
```

## Bulk seeding via looped requests

```js
before(() => {
  const employees = [
    { name: 'Aarav Sharma', department: 'Engineering' },
    { name: 'Bibek Thapa', department: 'HR' },
    { name: 'Chandra Rai', department: 'Finance' },
  ];

  employees.forEach((emp) => {
    cy.request('POST', '/api/employees', emp);
  });
});
```

Because Cypress commands are strictly serial (Part 2.3), this forEach correctly issues the requests one after another rather than firing them all simultaneously — no Promise.all() needed, and none would work correctly here anyway given the command queue's sequential nature.

## Retrying an API call until a condition is met

`cy.request()` itself doesn't retry (it's a one-shot HTTP call, unlike DOM queries), but wrapping it in .should() with a callback gives you exactly that — genuinely useful for polling a backend job:

```js
function waitForPayrollJob(jobId) {
  cy.request(`/api/payroll/jobs/${jobId}`).its('body.status').should('eq', 'completed');
}
```

Here, .should('eq', 'completed') retries the entire preceding command chain, including the `cy.request()` itself, because it's the query being retried per Part 2.4's rule — each retry attempt re-issues the HTTP call and re-checks the status. This is a clean way to poll a background job without a hand-rolled loop.

## Comparison

```js
// Cypress — request runs outside the browser page, but within the same Node-adjacent process
cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', payload).then((res) => { ... });

// Playwright — a fully separate, first-class APIRequestContext
const apiContext = await request.newContext({ baseURL: 'https://api.bizlevate.com' });
const response = await apiContext.post('/leave-requests', { data: payload });
expect(response.status()).toBe(201);

// Multiple independent, isolated contexts — each with its own cookies/auth
const adminContext = await request.newContext({ storageState: 'admin-auth.json' });
const managerContext = await request.newContext({ storageState: 'manager-auth.json' });
```

This is the concrete illustration of the scope gap flagged back in Part 0.2: Playwright's `APIRequestContext` is a genuinely separate object you create and manage, with its own isolated cookies and headers, and you can hold several simultaneously — useful for a test that needs to act as an admin and a manager in the same scenario without one login clobbering the other. `cy.request()` runs within your existing test's single session context; there's no built-in way to hold two independently-authenticated request contexts side by side. For the common case — seed some data before a UI test — this difference rarely matters. For scenarios genuinely requiring several simultaneous authenticated identities, Playwright's model has a real structural edge, and it's worth naming honestly in an interview rather than glossing over.

```python
# Selenium — no API testing capability whatsoever
# Teams pair Selenium with a completely separate tool: requests (Python), RestAssured (Java)
import requests
response = requests.post('https://api.bizlevate.com/leave-requests', json=payload)
assert response.status_code == 201
```

Selenium has zero built-in HTTP capability — it's purely a browser-automation tool. Any API work in a Selenium-based project happens through an entirely separate library, in the same language, run alongside but architecturally disconnected from the Selenium tests. This is a real, meaningful advantage for Cypress and Playwright both: API seeding and UI testing living in the same test file, the same language, and the same assertion vocabulary removes an entire category of cross-tool friction.

Verifying not just that an API response has the right values, but that it has the right shape — catching contract drift before it silently breaks the UI that consumes it.

## Why this matters beyond ordinary assertions

Ordinary Chai assertions (expect(response.body).to.have.property('id')) check specific fields you thought to check. Schema validation checks the entire shape of a response against a formal contract — catching cases like an unexpected extra field, a field silently changing from a number to a string, a required field going missing, or a nested object's structure shifting — all of which are exactly the kind of backend change that breaks a frontend without anyone touching the frontend code at all.

## The manual approach — Chai's structural assertions

For simple, stable response shapes, hand-written structural checks are often sufficient and require no new dependency:

```js
cy.request('/api/employees/123').then((response) => {
  expect(response.body).to.have.all.keys('id', 'name', 'email', 'department', 'joinDate', 'status');
  expect(response.body.id).to.be.a('number');
  expect(response.body.name).to.be.a('string');
  expect(response.body.email).to.match(/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/);
  expect(response.body.department).to.be.oneOf(['Engineering', 'HR', 'Finance', 'Sales']);
  expect(response.body.status).to.be.oneOf(['active', 'inactive', 'on-leave']);
});
```

have.all.keys is doing real work here — it fails if the response has any extra or missing top-level key, which is a genuinely strong contract check for a flat object. It becomes unwieldy fast for deeply nested or highly variable shapes, which is where a dedicated schema library earns its cost.

## JSON Schema validation with ajv

```bash
npm install --save-dev ajv

// cypress/support/schemas/employee.schema.js
module.exports = {
  type: 'object',
  required: ['id', 'name', 'email', 'department', 'joinDate', 'status'],
  additionalProperties: false,
  properties: {
    id: { type: 'number' },
    name: { type: 'string', minLength: 1 },
    email: { type: 'string', format: 'email' },
    department: { type: 'string', enum: ['Engineering', 'HR', 'Finance', 'Sales'] },
    joinDate: { type: 'string', format: 'date' },
    status: { type: 'string', enum: ['active', 'inactive', 'on-leave'] },
    manager: {
      type: ['object', 'null'],
      properties: {
        id: { type: 'number' },
        name: { type: 'string' },
      },
    },
  },
};
```

A custom Chai-style command wrapping ajv is the idiomatic way to make this feel native to Cypress's assertion style:

```js
// cypress/support/commands.js
const Ajv = require('ajv');
const addFormats = require('ajv-formats');
const ajv = new Ajv({ allErrors: true });
addFormats(ajv);

Cypress.Commands.add('validateSchema', { prevSubject: true }, (subject, schema) => {
  const validate = ajv.compile(schema);
  const valid = validate(subject);

  if (!valid) {
    const errors = validate.errors.map((e) => `${e.instancePath} ${e.message}`).join('\n');
    throw new Error(`Schema validation failed:\n${errors}`);
  }

  return subject;   // pass the subject through, so this chains like any other command
});

import employeeSchema from '../../support/schemas/employee.schema';

it('returns an employee matching the expected schema', () => {
  cy.request('/api/employees/123')
    .its('body')
    .validateSchema(employeeSchema);
});
```

Because validateSchema is registered with { `prevSubject`: true } (full custom command mechanics in Part 8.1) and returns the subject unchanged, it chains naturally and integrates into the Command Log like a built-in assertion — failures show the exact ajv error path, which is far more actionable than a generic "expected X to equal Y."

## Array/list response schemas

```js
const employeeListSchema = {
  type: 'array',
  items: employeeSchema,
  minItems: 0,
};

cy.request('/api/employees').its('body').validateSchema(employeeListSchema);
```

## Validating both request and response shapes together

```js
const createLeaveRequestSchema = {
  request: {
    type: 'object',
    required: ['employeeId', 'type', 'start', 'end'],
    properties: {
      employeeId: { type: 'number' },
      type: { type: 'string', enum: ['sick', 'annual', 'unpaid'] },
      start: { type: 'string', format: 'date' },
      end: { type: 'string', format: 'date' },
    },
  },
  response: {
    type: 'object',
    required: ['id', 'status', 'createdAt'],
    properties: {
      id: { type: 'number' },
      status: { type: 'string', const: 'Pending' },
      createdAt: { type: 'string', format: 'date-time' },
    },
  },
};

cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', payload).then((response) => {
  cy.wrap(response.body).validateSchema(createLeaveRequestSchema.response);
});
```

## Consuming an OpenAPI/Swagger spec directly — the highest-ceiling approach

For a backend that already publishes an OpenAPI spec, deriving your test's schema from the actual source of truth (rather than a hand-maintained duplicate that can silently drift from reality) is meaningfully more valuable:

```bash
npm install --save-dev openapi-response-validator js-yaml

// setupNodeEvents
const yaml = require('js-yaml');
const fs = require('fs');
const { OpenApiValidator } = require('express-openapi-validator');   // or a lighter validator lib

on('task', {
  getOpenApiSchema(operationId) {
    const spec = yaml.load(fs.readFileSync('openapi.yaml', 'utf8'));
    // extract the relevant response schema for the given operationId
    // (implementation depends on the chosen validator library)
    return extractSchemaFor(spec, operationId);
  },
});

cy.task('getOpenApiSchema', 'getEmployeeById').then((schema) => {
  cy.request('/api/employees/123').its('body').validateSchema(schema);
});
```

The genuine payoff here: if the backend team updates the OpenAPI spec but the actual endpoint drifts from it, this test catches the drift, not a hardcoded assumption baked into the test file. If the spec itself changes and the test schema is pulled from it live, the test stays honest about what "correct" currently means rather than testing against a schema someone wrote once and forgot to update.

## Contract testing as a category — a scoping note

Full consumer-driven contract testing (Pact, and similar tools that let a frontend team publish "here's what shape I expect" and have the backend's CI verify against it automatically) is a genuinely different discipline from what `cy.request()` + schema validation gives you. This chapter's approach validates a live, already-deployed API's actual response against a schema at test-run time — useful, real, and catches drift — but it's not the same guarantee as a formal contract test running in the backend's own pipeline before deployment. Worth knowing the distinction exists, even though building a full Pact-style contract testing setup is outside this manual's scope (flagged back in Part 0.8 as testing-philosophy territory this manual deliberately doesn't chase).

## Comparison

```js
// Cypress — via a custom command wrapping ajv, as shown above
cy.request('/api/employees/123').its('body').validateSchema(employeeSchema);

// Playwright — same idea, same ajv dependency, just invoked directly since test code is already in Node
const Ajv = require('ajv');
const ajv = new Ajv();
const validate = ajv.compile(employeeSchema);
const response = await apiContext.get('/employees/123');
const body = await response.json();
expect(validate(body)).toBeTruthy();
```

Neither tool has schema validation genuinely built in — both reach for the same underlying JS ecosystem library (ajv is the de facto standard either way), and the only difference is integration ergonomics: Cypress needs a small custom-command wrapper to make it chain naturally (Part 8), while Playwright's Node-native test code calls it directly with no wrapper needed. Functionally equivalent; a wash.

```python
# Selenium (paired with a Python API testing stack) — jsonschema is the equivalent library
import jsonschema
response = requests.get('https://api.bizlevate.com/employees/123')
jsonschema.validate(instance=response.json(), schema=employee_schema)
```

Selenium-paired API testing (via requests + jsonschema in Python, or RestAssured's built-in schema validation in Java) does this natively in whatever separate API-testing stack sits alongside the Selenium suite — RestAssured in particular has schema validation as a first-class, built-in feature (.body(matchesJsonSchema(...))), arguably more polished out of the box than either Cypress's or Playwright's ajv-based DIY approach, precisely because RestAssured was purpose-built as an API testing tool from the start rather than adapted from a browser-automation tool.

The strategic core of this entire Part — knowing when to test through the UI, when to test through the API, and how to blend both in a single scenario for maximum signal at minimum cost.

## The foundational principle: the testing pyramid, applied specifically to Cypress

Full testing-pyramid theory is out of scope per Part 0.8, but the practical, Cypress-specific version of the principle is essential here: every UI interaction in a test that isn't the actual thing under test is cost without benefit. It's slower, it's a point of potential flakiness (an extra selector that could change, an extra animation that could mistime), and it obscures what the test is actually verifying. The discipline this chapter teaches is asking, for every step in a test, "does this step need to go through the UI, or can it go through the API?"

## The decision framework

| If the test is about... | Drive it via... |
|---|---|
| The form/UI component itself (validation, field interactions, layout) | UI |
| Whether data appears correctly once loaded | API to seed, UI to verify display |
| A multi-step business flow where earlier steps aren't the focus | API for setup steps, UI for the step under test |
| Whether an API contract is correct | Pure API (6.1, 6.2) — no UI at all |
| An end-to-end smoke test proving the whole stack works together | Full UI, deliberately, as one of a small number of such tests |

## Pattern 1 — API to set up, UI to verify (the workhorse pattern)

This is the pattern threaded through 6.1 and reiterated here as the default habit:

```js
it('shows a manager exactly their team\'s pending leave requests', () => {
  cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', { employeeId: 101, managerId: 5, type: 'sick' });
  cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', { employeeId: 102, managerId: 5, type: 'annual' });
  cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', { employeeId: 201, managerId: 9, type: 'sick' });   // different manager

  cy.loginAs('manager5');
  cy.visit('/leave/approvals');

  cy.get('[data-cy=leave-row]').should('have.length', 2);   // only manager 5's team
  cy.get('[data-cy=leave-row]').should('not.contain', '201');
});
```

Three API calls replace what would otherwise be three complete trips through a multi-field submission form — and critically, the test is actually about the filtering logic on the approvals page, not about the submission form, so none of that setup should be visible in the test's failure surface at all.

## Pattern 2 — UI for the step under test, API for everything before and after it

```js
it('recalculates the leave balance immediately after approval', () => {
  cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', {
    employeeId: 101, type: 'annual', start: '2026-10-01', end: '2026-10-05',
  }).then((res) => {
    cy.request(`/api/employees/101/leave-balance`).its('body.annual').as('balanceBefore');

    cy.loginAs('manager');
    cy.visit(`/leave/approvals/${res.body.id}`);

    // THIS is the step under test — the UI approval action and its immediate effect
    cy.get('[data-cy=approve-btn]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=confirm]').click();
    cy.wait('@approveLeave');

    cy.get('@balanceBefore').then((before) => {
      cy.request(`/api/employees/101/leave-balance`).its('body.annual')
        .should('eq', before - 5);
    });
  });
});
```

Notice the final assertion goes back through the API, not the UI — because the thing being verified (the balance recalculation) is a backend concern, and asserting it via a direct API call is both faster and more precise than parsing a rendered number out of the DOM. The UI portion of this test is deliberately narrow: exactly the click-and-confirm sequence, nothing more.

## Pattern 3 — full UI, deliberately, for a small set of true end-to-end smoke tests

Not every test should follow the API-shortcut pattern. A handful of tests per feature area should exercise the real, complete path a user actually takes, precisely because they're the tests that catch integration bugs the shortcut patterns structurally cannot — a broken form field, a submit button wired to the wrong handler, a redirect that goes to the wrong place after a real login.

```js
it('[smoke] a new employee can be fully onboarded end to end', () => {
```
`cy.loginAs('admin')`;           // still via API for speed — this isn't testing login

```js
  cy.visit('/employees/onboard');

  // everything from here is genuinely full-UI, because the ONBOARDING FLOW is what's under test
  cy.get('[data-cy=first-name]').type('Aarav');
  cy.get('[data-cy=last-name]').type('Sharma');
  cy.get('[data-cy=next]').click();
  cy.get('[data-cy=department]').select('Engineering');
  cy.get('[data-cy=join-date]').type('2026-11-01');
  cy.get('[data-cy=next]').click();
  cy.get('[data-cy=finish]').click();

  cy.wait('@createEmployee');
  cy.url().should('match', /\/employees\/\d+$/);
  cy.get('[data-cy=employee-name]').should('contain', 'Aarav Sharma');
});
```

Note even this smoke test still uses API-based login — login itself isn't what's being smoke-tested here, onboarding is, so shortcutting the one piece that genuinely isn't the point remains correct even in an otherwise-full-UI test. The discipline isn't "never shortcut," it's "shortcut everything except the thing under test."
Tagging these as smoke tests (@smoke, covered fully in Part 10.1) lets a CI pipeline run the small, slow, high-value full-UI set on every commit while running the much larger API-shortcutted set for deeper coverage on a slower cadence, or vice versa depending on the team's risk tolerance.

## Pattern 4 — verifying the API call the UI makes, without faking the response

A blend distinct from both pure stubbing (5.1) and pure seeding: let a real backend handle the request, but assert on exactly what the UI sent it:

```js
it('sends the correct payload when approving with a comment', () => {
```
`cy.intercept('PUT', '/api/leave-requests/*/approve')`.as('approveLeave');   // spy, not stub — real backend responds

```js
  cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', { employeeId: 101, type: 'sick' }).then((res) => {
    cy.loginAs('manager');
    cy.visit(`/leave/approvals/${res.body.id}`);

    cy.get('[data-cy=approve-btn]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=comment]').type('Approved, please provide a doctor\'s note next time.');
    cy.get('[data-cy=confirm]').click();

    cy.wait('@approveLeave').its('request.body').should('deep.equal', {
      comment: "Approved, please provide a doctor's note next time.",
      notifyEmployee: true,
    });
  });
});
```

This confirms the frontend constructs the exact right request — a genuinely different, and complementary, concern from confirming the backend returns the right response shape (6.2) or that the UI renders correctly once data arrives (Pattern 1).

## Pattern 5 — race conditions and cross-cutting checks that need both layers simultaneously

```js
it('shows a conflict error if the request was already approved by another manager', () => {
  cy.request('POST', '/api/leave-requests', { employeeId: 101, type: 'sick' }).then((res) => {
    cy.loginAs('manager');
    cy.visit(`/leave/approvals/${res.body.id}`);

    // simulate a race: another manager approves it via the API while this page is open
    cy.request('PUT', `/api/leave-requests/${res.body.id}/approve`, { approverId: 999 });

    cy.get('[data-cy=approve-btn]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=confirm]').click();

    cy.get('[data-cy=conflict-error]')
      .should('be.visible')
      .and('contain', 'This request was already handled');
  });
});
```

This kind of test — verifying the UI handles a legitimate race condition gracefully — is close to impossible to write reliably through pure UI interaction (you can't easily get two browser tabs to race each other predictably in Cypress, per the multi-tab limitations from Part 0.6), but trivial once you accept that the "other manager" can simply be a direct API call happening mid-test.

## Anti-pattern: over-shortcutting to the point of testing nothing real

The discipline cuts both ways, and it's worth naming the failure mode at the other extreme explicitly:

```js
// ❌ This test verifies almost nothing about the actual application
it('shows the leave balance', () => {
  cy.intercept('GET', '/api/employees/101/leave-balance', { annual: 15 }); // fully stubbed
  cy.visit('/employees/101/leave-balance');   // and the rendering logic never touches real data shape
  cy.get('[data-cy=balance]').should('contain', '15');
});
```

If every test in a suite stubs every response, the suite verifies "does my component render the props I gave it" and nothing about whether the real backend's actual response shape matches what the frontend expects — which is precisely the kind of contract drift 6.2's schema validation exists to catch, and precisely why Pattern 3's genuine end-to-end smoke tests matter as a deliberate minority of the suite. The healthy suite is a mix: mostly API-seeded/UI-verified (Pattern 1, fast and focused), a meaningful set of pure API contract tests (6.1/6.2, fastest and most precise), and a small, deliberate set of true full-stack smoke tests (Pattern 3) that would catch the kind of integration failure the other two categories structurally cannot.

## Comparison

The strategic principle here — minimize UI interaction to exactly what's under test, use the API for everything else — applies identically in Playwright, using its `APIRequestContext` in exactly the Pattern 1/2 roles shown above. The capability gap noted in 6.1 (Playwright's ability to hold several independently-authenticated contexts at once) becomes concretely relevant here specifically for Pattern 5-style scenarios involving multiple simultaneous actors — Playwright can represent "manager A" and "manager B" as two fully separate, non-interfering request contexts with their own cookies, while Cypress's single shared session context makes this achievable (as shown above) but slightly more manual, since the "other manager's" API call has to be made without disturbing the primary session's own auth state, which `cy.request()`'s shared-context model handles fine for a single extra call but wouldn't scale cleanly to a scenario needing many simultaneous distinct identities.
Selenium-based projects implement this same strategic principle by pairing Selenium with a separate HTTP client library in the same test — the discipline of "seed via API, verify via UI" is tool-agnostic and predates all three of these frameworks; what differs is purely how ergonomically each tool lets you blend the two calls into one coherent test file. Cypress and Playwright both do this natively, in one file, one language, one assertion library — Selenium does it by importing two genuinely separate tools into the same test method.
